hi this is dr. Gregory Sadler I'm a  professor of philosophy and the  president and founder of an educational  consulting company called reason IO  where we put philosophy into practice  I've studied and taught philosophy for  over 20 years and I find that many  people run into difficulties reading  classic philosophical texts sometimes  it's the way things are said or how the  text is structured but the concepts  themselves are not always that  complicated and that's where I come in  to help students and lifelong learners  I've been producing longer lecture  videos and posting them to youtube many  viewers say they find them useful what  you're currently watching is part of a  new series of shorter videos each of  them focused on one core concept from an  important philosophical text I hope you  find it useful as well in his essay the  challenge of cultural relativism one of  the key issues that James Rachel's is  going to examine is how in whether we  can in fact criticize or even condemn  the social or cultural practices  belonging to other groups or cultures  and the example that he uses in this  text is female genital mutilation which  there he calls excision a very  controversial social practice widely  condemned in the West but widely upheld  and practiced in other parts of the  world so we'll come back to that example  but you should think of many other  similar examples of things which in one  culture are considered not only okay but  actually the norm what you ought to do  and then are condemned with in many  other cultures and we could do this  thinking about times as well something  that was okay in the 1950s in one  culture may not be okay in the new  millennium in that same although I in  certain ways changed culture so the  central question here is  should we say that practices in other  cultures are wrong are morally wrong  should we say that they ought to be  criticized or condemned or perhaps even  prevented or in some way you know  minimized or if we do that  are we being imperialists are we going  against what cultural relativism would  tell us is okay now it's unavoidable  that in saying that something is wrong  period in any case we are imposing some  standards even if we're doing it just  within our own society and even if we're  doing it in the crudest way imaginable  where you say I have the authority  therefore I say this is wrong or I just  feel that it is wrong there's still a  standard it's not a very good standard  but it's some sort of standard so we're  always imposing some sort of standards  the question is whether were legitimate  whether we're doing something right or  valid in imposing those standards so if  cultural relativism is actually right  then one of its key doctrines would be  that what we're doing in imposing these  standards is we're merely imposing the  standards of our own culture upon  members of another culture and then that  would be viewed as illegitimate as in  some sense wrong now there's some  problems there of course from what  perspective are you saying that's wrong  Rachel doesn't worry so much about that  because he has a good alternate line of  attack for this sort of thing and he  says that this is the issue of asking  whether there are any culture  independent standards that we could  appeal to is there a culture independent  standard of right and wrong he has it as  a heading and all we really need is at  least one for cultural relativism  not be holding uniformly through there  to be some sort of weakness to it so  again he looks at this this practice of  female genital mutilation and you can go  to people in the countries and cultures  where it is practiced and say listen  over here we think that's wrong and we  considered barbaric but I want to hear  things from your point of view why why  do you think that not only this is okay  but this is actually required why why do  you view this as a good thing and people  will give you answers and the answers  that they will give you will not just be  simply well that's our culture right  that may be the initial starting point  in a discussion but when you probe a  little bit deeper it doesn't take too  long to do this if you're actually  talking with people from from another  culture they will start giving you all  sorts of other justifications so for  example when it comes to female genital  mutilation you know there's a number of  arguments are made in its defense women  who are incapable of sexual pleasure or  less likely to be promiscuous thus there  will be fewer unwanted pregnancies and  unmarried women more over wives for whom  sex is only a duty or less likely to  cheat on their husbands because they're  not thinking about sex they will be more  attentive to the needs of their husbands  and children husbands for their part  aside to enjoy sex with wives who have  been excised on excised women the men  feel are unclean and immature and rachel  says it would be easy and perhaps  arrogant to ridicule these arguments  notice an important feature of every one  of these arguments get past the the sort  of repugnance that we might feel in  hearing this and see what's actually  going on here those who are justifying a  practice in their own culture are  appealing to some sort of social  standards something that doesn't just  depend on well we've always done things  this way or this is our culture it could  be if we don't do it the gods are gonna  go after us that is a standard right it  may not be a particularly good standard  in the long run but it's still a  standard  the one that Rachel's brings up is that  on the whole people are better off  that's what the the view point is if  this particular practice is encouraged  and continued and people will  accordingly be worse off if this  practice is not continued if it's if  it's prevented now once things get to  that point of view you're no longer  talking about something that is just  from the perspective of that culture  because the fact that an outsider can  come in and ask hey why are you doing  this  this seems wrong and they say no no  you've you've got a mistaken viewpoint  on it it's actually right they're not  saying it's just right for them they're  saying this is right morally for human  beings you just be with people over  there you've got it wrong you're missing  out some some sort of important  consideration and this is the the  standard so if you then talk about the  these standards like for example does  the practice promote or hinder the  welfare of people affected by it this  becomes as he says an independent moral  standard that cultural relativism  forbids and he says it's a standard that  can be brought to bear in judging the  practices of any culture at any time  including our own so you know let's  consider this issue of female genital  mutilation right you could say too  though those people listen when we  examine the people who have undergone  this it seems that psychologically they  wind up being damaged by it you don't  want people to be psychologically  damaged that's not to their welfare is  it so you know maybe you should stop  doing that practice or genuine intimacy  can take all these different forms and  shapes and depriving somebody of you  know you might call the the organ of  their intimacy or a portion of that is  interfering with that that possibility  of something that could be happy  in isn't and wouldn't that be conducive  to people's welfare as well and we could  do this with any other controversial  social practice that's found in one  culture and not in another culture we  could we could make it depend on welfare  we could make it depend on notions of  Rights we could make it depend on all  sorts of other moral principles that  would straddle and run through different  cultures Rachel's then discussed as well  why are we reluctant then since we have  all sorts of standards available to us  why are we reluctant to condemn the  practices of other cultures he says  there's actually some good reasons not  completely good reasons not reasons that  would actually militate against ever  condemning another culture but somewhat  good reasons for being reluctant to  condemn practices one particularly for  people in the West but really this could  apply to any dominant culture there are  concerns about interference with others  social customs with their sets of norms  do we actually have the right to do so  as he points out Europeans and their  descendants in America do have a  shameful history of destroying native  cultures in the name of Christianity and  enlightenment in a sort of religious and  a secular philosophical social point of  view right so you know we might be  concerned about continuing that and he  says that that does make sense and so  some people refuse to criticize other  cultures but he says there's a  difference between two things one is  making a judgment saying this is  actually wrong this is morally wrong  another is acting upon that judgment and  saying therefore we're going to send in  the troops or regulate you with law or  impose some sort of economic sanctions  or  something along those lines and although  it may often you know be the case that  doing a does lead to B we do have to  recognize that they're not equivalent  and they're not identical so there's  nothing wrong with condemning something  that we actually think is wrong as a  matter of fact that's what they do in  other cultures but we have to be wary  about what level of other actions we're  going to have flow from that  condemnation or criticism another thing  that he talks about here is valuing  tolerance positively people may feel and  he says rightly enough that we should be  tolerant of other cultures tolerance is  no doubt a virtue a tolerant person can  live in peace with those who see things  differently it doesn't require saying  everybody's right but it does say it  doesn't require saying I'm gonna live  with you and not engage in conflict with  you but that doesn't mean that we need  to be tolerant of everything and as he  points out no culture is actually  completely tolerant they all have some  things that they say are off limits so  he says if we did not think some things  are better than others there would be  nothing for us to tolerate then the  final thing that he talks about our  concerns that we might be expressing  contempt for somebody's entire culture  in criticizing a particular practice and  he says this is just a mistake  unless we are intending to criticize the  entire culture by pointing at look at  look at what these people do right we're  not actually doing that he says to  condemn a particular practice is not to  say the culture on the whole is  contemptible as a matter of fact though  the culture could have many admirable  features and you might actually use  those Admiral feet admirable features in  order to make the case to people of that  culture that they should reconsider the  practice that they are engaging in so  long story short from Rachel's point of  view we actually can and should if we're  going to be moral beings condemned  practices in other cultures that we  think are immoral of course we should  apply the same standard to ourselves but  we do have a sort of apparatus here in  social standards or universal standards  that go beyond those of a mere culture  we have something in those to appeal to  across different cultures  you
 

